	<item>
		<title>By: Frances Dickens</title>
		<link>http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=191&#038;cpage=1#comment-14526</link>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Frances Dickens]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Tue, 02 Apr 2013 07:46:54 +0000</pubDate>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://remembranceproject.wordpress.com/?page_id=191#comment-14526</guid>
		<description><![CDATA[I met Pir at Rabia Joyce Purcell&#039;s house in Wimbledon.  Pir gave a talk on &#039;Love&#039; and I know that his words reached every member of the room at all levels of understanding.  I had already &#039;met&#039; his father Hazrat Inayat Khan through his books of wisdom and I knew in my heart that I was a Sufi.  Joyce told me Pir would initiate me into the Sufi Order.  I entered the room and stood before him and looked into his eyes, they shone with love and light and my own eyes filled with tears, it was like &#039;coming home&#039;, difficult to explain but paradoxically simple.  What impressed me about Pir was his humility, I remember him saying &#039;don&#039;t worship me, the light comes through me, I am a channel.&#039; On another occasion Pir demonstrated the dervish dance steps and stumbled laughing.  He was easy to connect with, full of love, light and laughter.]]></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>I met Pir at Rabia Joyce Purcell&#8217;s house in Wimbledon.  Pir gave a talk on &#8216;Love&#8217; and I know that his words reached every member of the room at all levels of understanding.  I had already &#8216;met&#8217; his father Hazrat Inayat Khan through his books of wisdom and I knew in my heart that I was a Sufi.  Joyce told me Pir would initiate me into the Sufi Order.  I entered the room and stood before him and looked into his eyes, they shone with love and light and my own eyes filled with tears, it was like &#8216;coming home&#8217;, difficult to explain but paradoxically simple.  What impressed me about Pir was his humility, I remember him saying &#8216;don&#8217;t worship me, the light comes through me, I am a channel.&#8217; On another occasion Pir demonstrated the dervish dance steps and stumbled laughing.  He was easy to connect with, full of love, light and laughter.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
