	<item>
		<title>By: Eve Khushi Keller</title>
		<link>http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=191&#038;cpage=1#comment-3044</link>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Eve Khushi Keller]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Sun, 08 Nov 2009 16:36:43 +0000</pubDate>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://remembranceproject.wordpress.com/?page_id=191#comment-3044</guid>
		<description><![CDATA[Pir Vilayat:
His presence in this life is the greatest gift.
I met Pir Vilayat Khan in 1978 and received his initiation in Portland, Oregon, on February 27, 1979.
It was an initation indeed: my heart opened like a dam and love energy poured forth, unlike anything I had experienced before. In the following years I had many dreams of Pir, gifts of the inner life.
Last I saw Pir Vilayat was during Christmas Retreat in Germany, 1994. His glance very often rested on me and his aura appeared shining light to me. Around the time of his passing I felt an immensely deep attunement with prayer. When my own father died, in 2008, I was full of prayer for Pir to guide my dad. Somehow I feel he did.
I give great thanks and gratitude to my teacher-father. He has kindled the inner light and will always remain in this heart.
Toward the One,
Eve-Khushi]]></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Pir Vilayat:<br />
His presence in this life is the greatest gift.<br />
I met Pir Vilayat Khan in 1978 and received his initiation in Portland, Oregon, on February 27, 1979.<br />
It was an initation indeed: my heart opened like a dam and love energy poured forth, unlike anything I had experienced before. In the following years I had many dreams of Pir, gifts of the inner life.<br />
Last I saw Pir Vilayat was during Christmas Retreat in Germany, 1994. His glance very often rested on me and his aura appeared shining light to me. Around the time of his passing I felt an immensely deep attunement with prayer. When my own father died, in 2008, I was full of prayer for Pir to guide my dad. Somehow I feel he did.<br />
I give great thanks and gratitude to my teacher-father. He has kindled the inner light and will always remain in this heart.<br />
Toward the One,<br />
Eve-Khushi</p>
]]></content:encoded>
	</item>
	<item>
		<title>By: Michael O'Donnell</title>
		<link>http://remembrance.sufipaths.net/?page_id=191&#038;cpage=1#comment-2787</link>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Michael O'Donnell]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Wed, 23 Sep 2009 03:42:28 +0000</pubDate>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://remembranceproject.wordpress.com/?page_id=191#comment-2787</guid>
		<description><![CDATA[I am not someone who had a personal relationship with Pir for decades.  I am someone who met Pir, personally, once, for about sixty seconds, yet feel it is completely accurate to say Pir raised me, that he was, and is, the most important person in my life.  
   I have to say a bit about me to explain what I mean by this.  I met Pir when I was twenty-five.  For the previous twelve years, starting at about age thirteen,  I had suffered from a moderate to severe depression rooted in a difficult childhood and a biochemical disorder.  In 1951 my family was living in San Francisco and my alcoholic parents (my mother was also schizophrenic) would disappear for days at a time, leaving us (me, age three; my sister, age seven) to fend for ourselves.  We were eventually removed from their care and placed in Youth Guidance Center.  After about a year my sister and I were placed in a foster home.  Our foster parents were fine people, but my foster mother was severely depressed and my foster father was a typical working class male of his generation, that is, no inner life what ever.  On top of these sort of severe developmental challenges, I would discover that I suffered from bipolar disorder--not unusual for a child of a schizophrenic.
   I had, so far, managed to stay out of jail, the nut house, and off the street.  Other than that, it was a pillar-to-post existence.  Lacking a job with medical benefits, I sought out street pharmaceuticals to treat horrendous anxiety and depression. 
   The year I met Pir I was living in Albequerque.  My girlfriend wanted to drive to Tucson to pick up some belongings from her parent&#039;s house.  The night before we left I had a dream in which an East Indian man gave me a spiritual initiation.  When we arrived in Tucson my girlfriend decided to stay, so I told her I&#039;d spend the night and head back in the morning.  But when morning arrived I had the most incredible feeling of coming home--there was no question of leaving.  I found work and one day saw a poster for &quot;Sufi Dancing; Meditation In Motion.&quot;  The class was led by a man named Mansur, who shared the most beautiful prayers I&#039;d ever heard.  I started classes at a local center led by a man named Abduraqman.  A few months later, Pir came through town and I was initiated.  
   Within the year I moved back to the Bay Area, attended classes at the Mentogarden, and occasional retreats with Pir Vilayat.  What could prepare you for a retreat with Pir?  Who in the world could imagine a meeting of mind, heart and soul between two beings from such different backgrounds and circumstances?  
   I met Pir face to face in 3-D space for about sixty seconds, yet during retreats, where he was guiding 150-200 people at a time, he would suddenly appear before me in what I can only describe as some sort of non-local mindscape (he referred to it as &quot;inverted space) and grant me darshan.  I felt like a room he was perusing with an eye to improving the feng shui.  It&#039;s amazing how unremarkable it seemed at the time.  But even more amazing is how Pir managed to relate to someone from a background so vastly different from his own.  In time, Pir urged me to return to school, which I did.  That I eventually made the dean&#039;s list at a top university should be considered the kind of miracle that&#039;s grounds for canonization.  Perversely, the education I received from Pir made it difficult to embrace a career as an educator in American schools.  My life&#039;s work is now to write an integral, transformative pedagogy in the hope that, one day, schools might offer something like a real education.  
   So here&#039;s a man I spoke to once who literally raised me.  My story may not be common, but I doubt it&#039;s singular.  What I received from Pir informs every moment of every day of my life.  
But I also see that Pir&#039;s gifts to me came at a cost to others.  The sort of devotion Pir and his father exemplify takes a toll on their own families; a painful sacrifice for both parties, I&#039;m sure.  
   So this is both a memorial and an thank-you to Pir, as well as a thank you to Pir&#039;s children.  I wish there were words to tell you what your sacrifice has meant to me.]]></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>I am not someone who had a personal relationship with Pir for decades.  I am someone who met Pir, personally, once, for about sixty seconds, yet feel it is completely accurate to say Pir raised me, that he was, and is, the most important person in my life.<br />
   I have to say a bit about me to explain what I mean by this.  I met Pir when I was twenty-five.  For the previous twelve years, starting at about age thirteen,  I had suffered from a moderate to severe depression rooted in a difficult childhood and a biochemical disorder.  In 1951 my family was living in San Francisco and my alcoholic parents (my mother was also schizophrenic) would disappear for days at a time, leaving us (me, age three; my sister, age seven) to fend for ourselves.  We were eventually removed from their care and placed in Youth Guidance Center.  After about a year my sister and I were placed in a foster home.  Our foster parents were fine people, but my foster mother was severely depressed and my foster father was a typical working class male of his generation, that is, no inner life what ever.  On top of these sort of severe developmental challenges, I would discover that I suffered from bipolar disorder&#8211;not unusual for a child of a schizophrenic.<br />
   I had, so far, managed to stay out of jail, the nut house, and off the street.  Other than that, it was a pillar-to-post existence.  Lacking a job with medical benefits, I sought out street pharmaceuticals to treat horrendous anxiety and depression.<br />
   The year I met Pir I was living in Albequerque.  My girlfriend wanted to drive to Tucson to pick up some belongings from her parent&#8217;s house.  The night before we left I had a dream in which an East Indian man gave me a spiritual initiation.  When we arrived in Tucson my girlfriend decided to stay, so I told her I&#8217;d spend the night and head back in the morning.  But when morning arrived I had the most incredible feeling of coming home&#8211;there was no question of leaving.  I found work and one day saw a poster for &#8220;Sufi Dancing; Meditation In Motion.&#8221;  The class was led by a man named Mansur, who shared the most beautiful prayers I&#8217;d ever heard.  I started classes at a local center led by a man named Abduraqman.  A few months later, Pir came through town and I was initiated.<br />
   Within the year I moved back to the Bay Area, attended classes at the Mentogarden, and occasional retreats with Pir Vilayat.  What could prepare you for a retreat with Pir?  Who in the world could imagine a meeting of mind, heart and soul between two beings from such different backgrounds and circumstances?<br />
   I met Pir face to face in 3-D space for about sixty seconds, yet during retreats, where he was guiding 150-200 people at a time, he would suddenly appear before me in what I can only describe as some sort of non-local mindscape (he referred to it as &#8220;inverted space) and grant me darshan.  I felt like a room he was perusing with an eye to improving the feng shui.  It&#8217;s amazing how unremarkable it seemed at the time.  But even more amazing is how Pir managed to relate to someone from a background so vastly different from his own.  In time, Pir urged me to return to school, which I did.  That I eventually made the dean&#8217;s list at a top university should be considered the kind of miracle that&#8217;s grounds for canonization.  Perversely, the education I received from Pir made it difficult to embrace a career as an educator in American schools.  My life&#8217;s work is now to write an integral, transformative pedagogy in the hope that, one day, schools might offer something like a real education.<br />
   So here&#8217;s a man I spoke to once who literally raised me.  My story may not be common, but I doubt it&#8217;s singular.  What I received from Pir informs every moment of every day of my life.<br />
But I also see that Pir&#8217;s gifts to me came at a cost to others.  The sort of devotion Pir and his father exemplify takes a toll on their own families; a painful sacrifice for both parties, I&#8217;m sure.<br />
   So this is both a memorial and an thank-you to Pir, as well as a thank you to Pir&#8217;s children.  I wish there were words to tell you what your sacrifice has meant to me.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
